  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "One-Sided Laplace Transform Definition",
    "lesson_core_move": "Set up the one-sided Laplace transform as $X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$, identifying the lower limit, exponential weighting, and complex $s$-domain representation used for causal signal and system analysis.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "One-Sided Laplace Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to One-Sided Laplace Transform Definition",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In causal signal and system analysis, we start from right-sided behavior, so the one-sided Laplace transform is the direct map used when signals and inputs are modeled on $t \\ge 0^-$ rather than on the entire real line.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The complex frequency variable is written as $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$, and it is the independent frequency-domain coordinate that the time signal $x(t)$ is converted into.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=\\\\sigma+j\\\\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The one-sided definition is $X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$: we identify the time-domain signal $x(t)$, the exponential kernel $e^{-st}$, and the differential element $dt$ before evaluating anything.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $0^-$, not just $0$, so behavior at the origin is included correctly (such as impulses or jumps) while preserving the causal setup used for initial conditions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compared with the bilateral definition, the one-sided transform uses lower limit $0^-$ as its domain anchor, then keeps the same kernel and complex variable $s$, so the structural difference is the causal interval choice.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "One-Sided Laplace Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Time-Domain Signal and $s$-Domain Transform",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the one-sided Laplace setup for $x(t)=u(t)$ and identify the corresponding $X(s)$ setup notation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this worked example, identify the time-domain signal from the prompt and map it directly into the one-sided transform definition; the move is recognition first, setup second, no integration yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is $x(t)=u(t)$, which is causal by definition, so the target one-sided form still uses the same lower limit $0^-$ used in the section core move.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That expression is the required output: the time-domain signal is now written as $u(t)$, the weighting term is $e^{-st}$, and the differential is over $dt$ from $0^-$ to $\\infty$; this preserves the causal one-sided transform setup for later evaluation and for the upcoming multiple-choice practice.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "One-Sided Laplace Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Setting the One-Sided Integration Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set the integral limits for $X(s)$ when $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ and show why the lower limit is written $0^-$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The goal is to choose the one-sided integration interval before any algebraic simplification: the signal is causal and the transform setup must therefore keep the causal lower bound.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the prompt, identify the cue $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$, which is nonzero only for $t \\ge 0$, so the time-domain support implies the one-sided interval rule $t \\ge 0^-$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ into the definition and keep both $0^-$ and the exponential weighting unchanged in place.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-2t}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the final result as $X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-2t}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$. The $0^-$ lower limit is required so behavior at the origin is included in the causal convention used by the one-sided transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "One-Sided Laplace Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Applying the Exponential Weighting Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the one-sided integral for $x(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)$ showing the weighted integrand $x(t)e^{-st}$ clearly.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue now is $x(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)$, and the task is to apply the one-sided rule from earlier with the exponential weighting term explicit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(t)=3e^{-t}u(t)$ directly into the template and multiply by the kernel $e^{-st}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} 3e^{-t}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine exponentials to make the weighted integrand explicit before integration: $3e^{-t}e^{-st}=3e^{-(s+1)t}$, so the setup is $3u(t)e^{-(s+1)t}$ inside the one-sided integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} 3e^{-(s+1)t}u(t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final weighted-setup result is $X(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} 3e^{-(s+1)t}u(t)\\,dt$, with bounds unchanged at $0^-$ and $\\infty$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
